          "number": 14,
          "letter": "N",
          "english": "Is the state-by-state regulatory system outdated? Historically, regulation of medicine has been left to individual states. But some industry members contend that having 50 different sets of rules, licensing fees and even definitions of \"medical practice\" makes less sense in the era of telemedicine and is hampering its growth. Currently, doctors must have a valid license in the state where the patient is located to provide medical care, which means virtual-visit companies can match users only with locally licensed clinicians. It also causes administrative hassles(麻烦) for world-class medical centers that attract patients from across the country. At the Mayo Clinic, doctors who treat out-of-state patients can follow up with them via phone, email or web chats when they return home, but they can only discuss the conditions they treated in person. \"If the patient wants to talk about a new problem, the doctor has to be licensed in that state to discuss it. If not, the patient should talk to his primary-care physician about it,\" says Steve Ommen, who runs Mayo's Connected Care program.",
          "chinese": "按州分别监管的制度是否已经过时？从历史上看，医疗监管一直由各州单独负责。但一些行业人士认为，在远程医疗时代，拥有50套不同的规则、许可费用，甚至对“医疗实践”的不同定义，已经不再合理，并且正在阻碍远程医疗的发展。目前，医生必须持有患者所在州的有效执照，才能提供医疗服务；这意味着虚拟问诊公司只能为用户匹配持有当地执照的临床医生。这也给吸引全国各地患者的世界一流医疗中心带来了行政上的麻烦。在梅奥诊所，治疗外州患者的医生可以在患者回家后通过电话、电子邮件或网络聊天跟进病情，但他们只能讨论当面治疗过的疾病。梅奥诊所“互联医疗”项目负责人史蒂夫·奥曼说：“如果患者想谈论一个新问题，医生必须持有该州的执照才能讨论。如果没有，患者就应该就此咨询自己的初级保健医生。”",
